 Next.js vendors an internal build of react-server-dom-webpack. Its Flight
 reply decoder (decodeReply/initializeModelChunk) calls JSON.parse on
 attacker-controlled request bodies with no bound on structural nesting
 depth, letting a crafted payload with extreme array/object nesting burn
 large amounts of CPU during parsing and cause a denial of service. This
 patch adds enforceModelStringLimits, an O(n) string-literal-aware scanner
 that walks the raw JSON text counting bracket/brace nesting depth (skipping
 characters inside string literals and honoring backslash escapes) before
 JSON.parse runs, throwing once depth exceeds a configurable limit
 (response._arraySizeLimit, default 1000) or, when a limit is explicitly
 configured, once the cumulative structural node count across chunks
